skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- enact ---
task: The spec for uppercase-flag is written and I've read it — start the build. Implement it and get it ready for me to look at.
--- setup ---
git init -q
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# greet-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: greet-cli

## Mission
A tiny shell greeting tool.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/greet.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/greet.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `reviewer: host-native`
- `gates:`
  - `dev:`
    - `quality: sh lint.sh`
    - `tests: sh test.sh`
MD
cat > specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md <<'MD'
# Feature: uppercase flag

Branch: feat/uppercase-flag

## Feature Brief
`greet.sh` gains a `-u` flag that upper-cases the greeting.

## Requirements
- R1. `greet.sh World` prints `Hello, World!` as today.
- R2. `greet.sh -u World` prints `HELLO, WORLD!`.

## Design
Parse `-u` as the first argument; pipe the composed line through `tr`.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts both R1 and R2.

## Handoff

- **Ready for:** dev, once the plan is approved.
- **Carry forward:** `tr` is available; no new dependency needed.
- **Deliberately left:** long-form `--upper` alias — `-u` only for now.

## Implementation Plan
- [ ] 1 · Add `-u` parsing to `src/greet.sh`.
- [ ] 2 · Add the R2 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log

## Decision Log
MD
cat > src/greet.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
echo "Hello, $1!"
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/greet.sh World)
[ "$out" = "Hello, World!" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/greet.sh test.sh lint.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(uppercase-flag): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/uppercase-flag
# Wire the phase tooling the way install does, so the agent inherits a
# project where the hooks are already live.
"$KDEVKIT_TOOLS/phase" install >/dev/null
--- assert ---
set -e
# The dev work actually happened and the old behaviour still holds.
out=$(sh src/greet.sh World); [ "$out" = "Hello, World!" ]
out=$(sh src/greet.sh -u World); [ "$out" = "HELLO, WORLD!" ]
grep -qF 'HELLO, WORLD!' test.sh
grep -qF -e '-u' test.sh
sh test.sh >/dev/null
# Plan items are ticked, which the dev gate requires before review.
test -z "$(grep -F -e '- [ ]' specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md || true)"
grep -qF -e '- [x]' specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md
# The stage is recorded in git, by the hook, without the agent being
# asked to remember. A no-op agent leaves only the seeded plan() commit,
# which carries no stage at all because the hooks were wired after it.
stage=$(git log --format='%(trailers:key=Feature-Phase,valueonly=true,unfold=true)' | grep . | head -1 | tr -d '[:space:]')
test -n "$stage"
# ...and it must have moved past planning, which is what the dev phase does.
test "$stage" != "planning"
# The recorded stage is a legal value, not junk or the template's list.
printf '%s' "$stage" | grep -qE '^(dev|review)$'
# Dev-loop churn must not read as going backwards.
test "$(git log --format='%(trailers:key=Return-To,valueonly=true,unfold=true)' | grep -c . || true)" = "0"
# Dev-type commits exist — an agent still in planning would produce plan().
git log --no-decorate --oneline feat/uppercase-flag | grep -qE ' (feat|fix|refactor|test)(\(|:)'
# Closure did not run: no explicit cue was given.
test -z "$(git log --no-decorate --oneline feat/uppercase-flag | grep -E '^[[:alnum:]]+ close\(' || true)"
# The handoff block keeps exactly one section and no machine field, since
# the stage now lives in git rather than in prose.
test "$(grep -c '^## Handoff' specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md)" = 1
